e.py` (reason codes, `Refusal`, `FrozenError`, `Outcome`), `policy.py` (the committed-policy parser, whose fence helpers are the canonical patch seams), `runtime.py` (the one canonical module for the patchable controls — the clock, the bounded process runner, the merge lifecycle flag, the Revision-9 state controls, the path roots, the lazily imported coordination modules, the fast-tier mechanical-skip predicate, and the late-bound journal-record builder seam — which every module of the split reads by attribute so one in-memory patch on `forge_cli.runtime` disables a control everywhere), `candidate.py` (the shared bounded, pinned Git-context implementation for immutable tree snapshots, deterministic review evidence, marker identity, and raw produced-commit inspection), the `chain_core/` package (`scripts/forge/forge_cli/chain_core/`, whose root `__init__.py` holds the verbatim `__all__` and re-exports every symbol the historical module defined (its unused imports are not re-bound on the root), and whose modules — the root `__init__.py` and the submodules, together exactly the `scripts/forge/forge_cli/chain_core/` production subjects listed in `.forge/evals/tasks/fr230-phase3-4-v2.manifest.json` — hold the fenced process runner, the FR-235 common-lock arbiter and chain leases, chain and merge-chain storage, merge state and transition validation, the ingest verifiers, and the Revision-9 seam-marker loop (in `_commit_chain.py` beside `register_coordination_seams`, with `_seam` re-exported on the root for attribute parity)), the `engine/` package (`scripts/forge/forge_cli/engine/`, whose root `__init__.py` holds the verbatim `__all__` and re-exports every symbol the historical `engine.py` defined (its unused imports are not re-bound on the root), and whose submodules — the root and the submodules together exactly the `scripts/forge/forge_cli/engine/` production subjects listed in `.forge/evals/tasks/fr230-phase3-4-v2.manifest.json` — hold the `Engine` commit-chain class (`_engine.py`), argument-parser construction, the finalize pipeline, review transport, approval, secret-scan and fresh-eval helpers, the archive surface, merge admission/scope/claim/epoch/conflict/worktree helpers, and the Revision-12 bootstrap child protocol; `_journal.py` binds the journal-record builder onto the runtime seam at import, and the bootstrap child re-execs `scripts/forge/cli.py` resolved from `runtime.py`'s own location), and `app.py` (`MergeEngine`, the shared chain-verb router, argument-parsing and dispatch entry points, and `main`; it obtains the parser and other engine helpers through `forge_cli.engine`). The existing FR-230 phase-3/4 manifest retains its historical production subjects; every changed or added production subject, including `candidate.py`, requires a current applicable Recorded-baseline integrity (`strict-evals`) result binding, plus `fresh-reviewer-evals` when the authenticated-base trigger matches; an unverifiable trigger blocks through its required INVALID path. The split changes no verb, diagnostic, reason code, `--help` byte, or immutable prior corpus.\n   118\t- `scripts/forge/forge_cli/fresh_evals.py` — the non-executable, Python-interpreter-loaded internal collector and verifier for candidate-bound fresh reviewer evaluation; behind the chain-owned `engine.py` launcher adapter it implements authenticated trigger derivation, candidate-tree fixture inventory and oracle withholding, bounded native child execution, canonical request-bound artifacts, manifest verification, and the current-candidate PASS predicate without adding a second CLI entry point\n   119\t- `scripts/forge/cli.py` — the Forge CLI commit-chain slice (FR-210..FR-224): a persisted chain state machine invoked as `python3 \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/forge/cli.py\" <verb>`, owning commit-chain sequencing, staging, candidate identity, evidence capture, and finalize. No PATH binary named `forge` is installed (the Foundry `forge` collision); `forge <verb>` anywhere in this spec is shorthand for that invocation form. Phase-gated: ships only after the FR-223 precondition evals pass\n   120\t- Phase-3 Forge CLI merge surfaces specified by FR-230..FR-237: `status`, `merge start|refresh|verify|gate run|approve|finalize|recover|cleanup|abort`, and the shared `review request|attach|disposition` verbs. These are normative future surfaces, not implementation delivered by Revision 7.\n   121\t- Phase-4 `forge push`, DM-015 activation parsing, and the v2 PreToolUse matcher/raw-denial behavior specified by FR-238..FR-241. These are normative future surfaces, not implementation delivered by Revision 7.\n   122\t- Additive phase-3/4 v2 reason-code and hook-vector artifacts in the exact DM-016 inventory. Every v1 artifact remains immutable.\n   123\t- Revision-9 typed writer, batch recovery, structured binding, ingest, chain-evidence, and archive-rerender contracts; the additive v3 reason-code corpus extends only `forge-cli/2` and preserves every v1/v2 row byte-identically.\n   124\t\n   125\t### Modified surfaces\n   270\tenforces the executable-fence boundary rather than interpreting those prose labels.\n   271\t\n   272\tThe `reviewer-facing-eval-triggers` region is the sole maintained reviewer-facing trigger path list and contains exactly these ordered rows:\n   273\t\n   274\t| control | path patterns |\n   275\t|---|---|\n   276\t| constitution | rules/** |\n   277\t| agent-prompt-template | agents/**, system/codex/prompts/**, .claude/agents/** |\n   278\t| reviewer-routing | system/codex/agents/**, system/codex/config.toml, .codex/agents/**, .codex/config.toml, skills/orchestrate/SKILL.md, scripts/forge/forge_cli/engine/** |\n   279\t| execpolicy | system/codex/rules/**, .codex/rules/** |\n   280\t| model-provider-version | docs/specs/forge-plugin-spec.md, agents/**, system/codex/agents/**, .codex/agents/**, skills/orchestrate/SKILL.md, scripts/forge/forge_cli/engine/** |\n   281\t| commit-review-prompt | skills/commit/SKILL.md |\n   282\t\n   283\tEach path cell is a comma-separated list of positive repository-relative Git pathspec globs. The complete LF-terminated table above is one canonical parser constant: acceptance requires byte-for-byte equality, so even a well-formed row substitution or narrowing is malformed. The template and root region carry those same bytes, and `/forge:init` retains an exact match but refreshes any noncanonical current-format body from the canonical template on reinstall. The table is therefore also malformed if it is missing or repeated, its header or row order differs, a control label is unknown or repeated, a path cell is empty, or a pattern is empty, repeated within its row, absolute, negative, magic-prefixed, contains `..` as a path component, or contains NUL, CR, or LF. Repetition of one pattern in different control rows is permitted and every matching `(control, pattern, path)` tuple is evidence. Matching uses only the bytewise-sorted exact path set derived from the DM-012 base-tree/candidate-tree comparison; it never uses caller-supplied targets, `git status`, or working-tree names. The region and its digest are loaded from the chain's authenticated base-policy bytes, so candidate edits cannot narrow their own trigger. For a control-class candidate, a missing, repeated, malformed, unreadable, or unauthenticatable base region is mandatory fresh-evaluation applicability with an INVALID result, not an empty match set or an unconfigured gate; it takes FR-220's fresh-evaluation exit-2 transport. Only a successfully authenticated and strictly parsed region whose derived match array is empty makes `fresh-reviewer-evals` unconfigured. First-policy bootstrap instead takes FR-083's unconditional fixed-policy path.\n   284\t\n   285\tFor the current implementation, the `scripts/forge/forge_cli/engine/` package (`scripts/forge/forge_cli/engine/**` in the fixed table; the `Engine` class in `_engine.py`, the fresh-eval request and evidence helpers in `_fresh_eval.py` and `_fresh_eval_evidence.py`) remains the chain-owned launcher adapter named by the fixed table: it owns gate routing, request-event persistence, launch admission, and terminal step publication. An interpreter-loaded helper may implement bounded collection mechanics but does not replace that adapter or become a second maintained trigger list.\n   286\t\n   287\tInside the `risk-tiers` region, the template MUST ship this plugin-owned block verbatim; `/forge:init` MUST preserve it when filling or refreshing the surrounding region. It is part of committed `forge-project.md`, and therefore control-class. The classifier and guard consume this same committed block under FR-151/FR-154 rather than maintaining separate manifest lists:\n   288\t\n   289\t```text\n   290\t<!-- FORGE:DEPENDENCY-MANIFEST-PATHS BEGIN -->\n   291\tpackage.json\n   292\tpackage-lock.json\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
